answer_text:
- Purpose: codes of conduct may specify the application of the Regulation (G Art. 40(2)).

- Elements that such a code should/may specify (G Art. 40(2)(a)–(k)):
  - fair and transparent processing (G Art. 40(2)(a));
  - the legitimate interests pursued by controllers in specific contexts (G Art. 40(2)(b));
  - the collection of personal data (G Art. 40(2)(c));
  - the pseudonymisation of personal data (G Art. 40(2)(d));
  - the information provided to the public and to data subjects (G Art. 40(2)(e));
  - the exercise of the rights of data subjects (G Art. 40(2)(f));
  - the information provided to, and the protection of, children, and how parental consent is to be obtained (G Art. 40(2)(g));
  - the measures and procedures referred to in Articles 24 and 25 and the measures to ensure security of processing referred to in Article 32 (G Art. 40(2)(h));
  - the notification of personal data breaches to supervisory authorities and the communication of such breaches to data subjects (G Art. 40(2)(i));
  - the transfer of personal data to third countries or international organisations (G Art. 40(2)(j));
  - out-of-court proceedings and other dispute‑resolution procedures between controllers and data subjects (without prejudice to Articles 77 and 79) (G Art. 40(2)(k)).

- Other relevant points:
  - Codes should take account of the specific features of processing sectors and the specific needs of micro, small and medium‑sized enterprises (G Art. 40(1)).
  - When drawing up, amending or extending a code, associations and bodies should consult relevant stakeholders, including data subjects where feasible, and have regard to submissions and views received (G Rec. 99).

A qualified electronic signature is validated by a process that confirms the signature is valid only if all of the following are met (E Art. 32.1):
- the certificate supporting the signature was, at the time of signing, a qualified certificate complying with Annex I (E Art. 32.1(a); E Art. 28.1);  
- the qualified certificate was issued by a qualified trust service provider and was valid at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(b));  
- the signature validation data corresponds to the data provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(c));  
- the unique set of data representing the signatory in the certificate is correctly provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(d));  
- the use of any pseudonym is clearly indicated to the relying party if a pseudonym was used at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(e));  
- the electronic signature was created by a qualified electronic signature creation device (E Art. 32.1(f); E Art. 30.1);  
- the integrity of the signed data has not been compromised (E Art. 32.1(g)); and  
- the requirements of Article 26 were met at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(h)). Article 26(26) requires that an advanced electronic signature be uniquely linked to and capable of identifying the signatory, be created using signature-creation data the signatory can use under his sole control, and be linked to the signed data so any subsequent change is detectable (E Art. 26.26).

Operational and service requirements for validation:
- the validation system must provide the relying party the correct result and allow detection of any security‑relevant issues (E Art. 32.2).  
- the Commission may specify reference standards for validation by implementing acts; compliance with Article 32(1) is presumed where validation meets those standards (E Art. 32.3).  
- a qualified validation service may be offered only by a qualified trust service provider that performs validation in compliance with Article 32(1) and allows relying parties to receive the validation result in an automated, reliable and efficient manner bearing the provider’s advanced electronic signature or seal (E Art. 33.1).

An electronic signature qualifies as an "advanced electronic signature" when it meets all of the following requirements (E Art. 26):
- it is uniquely linked to the signatory (E Art. 26);  
- it is capable of identifying the signatory (E Art. 26);  
- it is created using electronic signature creation data that the signatory can, with a high level of confidence, use under his sole control (E Art. 26); and  
- it is linked to the data signed therewith in such a way that any subsequent change in the data is detectable (E Art. 26).

Compliance with those requirements is presumed where the advanced electronic signature meets standards referenced in implementing acts established by the Commission (E Art. 27.4).
